  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Saddle-shaped False Morel (Paragyromitra infula) is a distinctive fungus featuring a dark brown to blackish cap with a characteristic saddle-like or irregular wavy shape. Despite its name, it is not a true morel and belongs to the Discinaceae family, containing compounds that can be toxic if improperly prepared.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Saddle-shaped Cap",
      "description": "The cap has a distinctive saddle or wave-like appearance with irregular lobes, unlike the pitted honeycomb structure of true morels.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Dark Coloration",
      "description": "The cap is typically dark brown to blackish, often with a wrinkled or brain-like texture on the underside.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Chambered Interior",
      "description": "When cut in half lengthwise, the interior is chambered or compartmented rather than completely hollow like true morels.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Paragyromitra infula grows in coniferous and mixed forests throughout New England, typically appearing in fall on soil, leaf litter, and near rotting wood.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being called a 'false morel,' Paragyromitra infula contains gyromitrin, a toxic compound that can cause serious illness; however, some cultures parboil and dry them to reduce toxicity before consuming them.",
